## GraphQL Subscriptions

## How They Work

```
1. Client opens WebSocket: ws://api.example.com/graphql
2. Client sends subscription:
   subscription {
     messageAdded(channelId: "general") {
       id
       text
       author { name }
       createdAt
     }
   }
3. Server acknowledges subscription
4. When a new message is added → server pushes update to all subscribers
5. Client receives real-time update without polling
```

## Server Implementation (Yoga + Redis Pub/Sub)

```typescript
import { createPubSub } from 'graphql-yoga';
import { createClient } from 'redis';

const pubSub = createPubSub();

const resolvers = {
  Mutation: {
    sendMessage: async (_, { channelId, text }, { userId }) => {
      const message = await db.createMessage({ channelId, text, authorId: userId });
      // Publish to all subscribers of this channel
      pubSub.publish(`MESSAGE_ADDED_${channelId}`, { messageAdded: message });
      return message;
    },
  },
  Subscription: {
    messageAdded: {
      subscribe: (_, { channelId }) =>
        pubSub.subscribe(`MESSAGE_ADDED_${channelId}`),
      resolve: (payload) => payload.messageAdded,
    },
  },
};
```

## Client Side (React + Apollo)

```typescript
const MESSAGES_SUBSCRIPTION = gql`
  subscription OnMessageAdded($channelId: ID!) {
    messageAdded(channelId: $channelId) {
      id text createdAt
      author { name }
    }
  }
`;

function Chat({ channelId }: { channelId: string }) {
  const { data } = useSubscription(MESSAGES_SUBSCRIPTION, {
    variables: { channelId },
  });
  // data.messageAdded updates in real-time
}
```

## When Not To Use Subscriptions

```
Polling is fine for:  Updates every few minutes, non-critical freshness
SSE is simpler for:  One-way server push (notifications, progress bars)
Subscriptions for:   Bidirectional, typed, high-frequency real-time data
```

## API Security Checklist

## Input Validation

```typescript
// Limit payload size (prevent DoS)
export const config = { api: { bodyParser: { sizeLimit: '1mb' } } };

// Validate all inputs — never trust the client
const schema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email().max(254),
  comment: z.string().max(1000).trim(),
});
```

## Injection Prevention

```typescript
// ❌ SQL Injection risk
const query = `SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '${email}'`;

// ✅ Parameterised query
const result = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1', [email]);

// ✅ ORM (Prisma) — never builds raw SQL from user input
const user = await prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email } });
```

## Security Headers (Next.js)

```typescript
// next.config.ts
const headers = [
  { key: 'Strict-Transport-Security', value: 'max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains' },
  { key: 'X-Content-Type-Options', value: 'nosniff' },
  { key: 'X-Frame-Options', value: 'DENY' },
  { key: 'Referrer-Policy', value: 'strict-origin-when-cross-origin' },
  { key: 'Permissions-Policy', value: 'camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()' },
];
```

## JWT Hardening

```typescript
// ✅ RS256 over HS256 — asymmetric: sign with private key, verify with public key
// Even if your public key leaks, attackers can't sign new tokens

// ✅ Validate all standard claims
jwt.verify(token, PUBLIC_KEY, {
  algorithms: ['RS256'],
  audience: 'interview-prep-api',
  issuer: 'auth.yourapp.com',
});

// ✅ Short expiry on access tokens (15 min)
// ✅ Refresh token rotation (issue new refresh on each use)
// ✅ Maintain a refresh token denylist in Redis for logout
```

